EVAN'S METHOD: TARGET, TEMPO, TEAM — Virtual EM Knowledge Base
Source: Evan's recent trainings on the three beliefs that keep creatives stuck, the three pillars of releasing creative work, and the sinkhole that affects mid-career creative professionals.

HOW TO USE THIS
Reach for a tool only when the member's situation calls for it. Name it plainly, define it in one sentence, offer one step. Never walk through the whole model. Never promise outcomes. The six-week and twelve-week figures describe what usually happens in Evan's work, not a guarantee for the member.

THE GOAL: THE THRIVING ARTIST
There are two ends of a spectrum. The Starving Artist has creative freedom, or thinks so, but no financial stability, so they aren't really free. The Successful Sellout has income but misses the full wealth of a creative practice that feeds them. The thriving artist lives in the Goldilocks zone between the two. Where that sits varies from person to person.

THREE BELIEFS THAT KEEP CREATIVES STUCK, AND THE FLIP FOR EACH
Target. Belief: "I need clarity before I can act." Flip: get honest first. Honesty about where you really are and what you really want opens up clarity, which sits downstream of it. Momentum does the same job: quick wins and action bring the clarity. Sometimes you don't know what you want until you go after it.
Tempo. Belief: "I need more time." Flip: you need more energy. Everybody has the same 24 hours. Stop looking for free time to do your best work. Look for energy.
Team. Belief: "I have to figure out how first." Flip: ask who. Who can help you get where you want to go? A related belief is "I need more motivation." What people usually need is a team. A big, bold vision isn't meant to stay with you alone, even if you're a solo artist.

TARGET TOOLS
Drop a Pin. Get a bead on the member's current location: their situation, through intake questions and a deep dive. Clarity about where you are matters, but it isn't the most important thing.
The 90-day target. Twelve weeks is close enough to keep you in motion and feel urgent, and far enough to let things percolate.
Cover the Canvas (from Steven Pressfield). Give yourself permission to make a crummy first draft and put it out. It tells you where to go and gets you over the hump of needing it all figured out. Refine it over the next season.
Truth Serum. For the move from clarity to honesty. Two parts:
- Body Map. An initial assessment distilled from over a year of therapy (Evan is not a therapist). Get in touch with the facts and feelings of where you are right now, locate them in the body, and let the body tell you the truth. The body doesn't lie. Use it for a stuck project or a project you're unsure how to move forward.
- Hot Wash. Borrowed from after-action reviews in special operations. Every seven days: what worked, what didn't, what you said you wanted to get done, what pivots you want to make. Getting honest with yourself every seven days lets you adjust as you go.

TEAM TOOLS
Parking Brake. Even with a compelling target, there is often a part of you unconsciously resisting, a parking brake on while you push forward. Instead of chasing motivation, make sure all the parts of you are on board and become your own best teammate. A self-guided journaling exercise helps you let go of the brake.
The Inner Circle. The people you're in touch with often who encourage you, guide you, keep you accountable, and watch out for your mental health. One seat is yourself, being your own best ally. Audit who sits in each seat. Start with the one seat that would have the biggest ripple impact.
Epic Partnerships and the Street Team. Not just people handing out flyers. These are promotional partners, collaborators, patrons, and supporters. Pick one bucket, focus there, and work out who can help the member toward the project and the goal. It's never a question of how. It's a question of who.

TEMPO TOOLS
Sacred Margin. Before adding anything, preserve margin. Eliminate, delegate, say no. Protect time for deep work on the art and space for ideas to incubate and marinate: a walk, a day unplugged from screens.
The Battery Check. Two parts:
- A streak of sacred studio time, even 15 to 20 minutes a day, learned from stand-up comedians. Don't break the streak. Anchoring those minutes to your best work feeds you energy instead of draining it.
- An audit of the Core Four for energy leaks: Body (physical health), Being (spiritual and inner life), Bonds (close relationships), Business (money and career work around the art). Pick one keystone habit and keep resetting it weekly and daily.
The retrospective. In week six, look at what's working and what isn't. Install the habit of adjusting, because the target might shift, the team might change, or a season might demand a faster or slower tempo.

THE PATH IN BRIEF
Target comes first. Team and Tempo follow. Evan's experience is that most projects reach a first draft in about six weeks and a solid section released in about twelve, and some take longer or shorter. Say this as Evan's experience, never as a promise.

THE SINKHOLE
Mid-career creative professionals in institutional roles (nonprofit, church, arts organization, freelance) often keep meeting every outside deadline while their own creative work quietly slips. What used to fill them gets pushed aside by admin, commitments, and management. It's subtle, and it's where the battle is won or lost.
Early signs:
- Saying "not yet" or "not now" to their own boldest work.
- Hedging, censoring, or caveating their own ideas.
- Hiding what they're making, or dampening their enthusiasm.
- Hesitating to share.
- Analysis paralysis, over-preparing, procrastinating, revision hell.
Outward metrics can look fine. Left alone, engagement erodes quietly, like a sinkhole, until an ordinary stress tips it into burnout or an exit.
The way out is putting the creative work first. When the boldest work sits in "someday," it gets treated like entertainment, an escape, or a side thing. It is actually core identity work that sustains a life in the arts, not a luxury. Saying yes might look like the project on the back burner, the demo at 70 percent, the workshop, the chapter.
For Virtual EM: name the early sign you hear in the member's own words and ask whether it's already happening. Then find the biggest bottleneck keeping the work on the back burner and name one concrete next action. Take it off the hard drive and out of the voice memos.

DR. MVP CHECKLIST (Evan's adaptation)
Check each post or invitation against these five elements:

Description — who, what, where.
Relevance — how it connects to their actual life: family, finances, health, fun.
Meaning — what attending or engaging does for someone (a break from the chaos of life).
Value — why it's rare, important, or scarce.
Purpose — from the mundane (catch your breath) to the existential (ecology, mindfulness).

Telling too much: share the Description, the Perspective, and the Process. Don't try to share the experience itself. A video can't give someone Meaning or Value — keep your emphasis there.

